readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|By one death a thousand deaths we slay;<br>''There-by'' we rise from body-Toomb of Clay;<br>''There-by'' our Soules feast with celestiall food;<br>''There-by'' we com to th' heav'nly Brother-hood.
{{right|''Sylvester'', tr. of Du Bartas's Weeks, ii., The Decay.}}}}

2. Annexed to that; in that connection.

{{smaller block|''Quick.'' Have not your worship a wart above your eye?<br>''Fent.'' Yes, marry, have I; what of that?<br>''Quick.'' Well, ''thereby'' hangs a tale.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. W. of W., i. 4. 159.}}}}

3. By or near that place; near that number, quantity, or degree.

{{smaller block|''Therby'' ys an other howse that sumtyme was a fayer Churche of Seynt Anne.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 31.}}
I . . . found a chapel, and ''thereby''<br>A holy hermit in a hermitage.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}

¶
'''therefor''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-fôr′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therfor''; a form of ''therefore'', now used only as if a modern formation, < ''there'' + ''for'', for that: see ''therefore''.] For this or for that; for it: as, the building and so much land as shall be necessary ''therefor''.

'''therefore''' (in defs. 1, 2, 3, ᴛ̵ʜãr-fōr′; in def. 4, ᴛ̵ʜãr′fōr, sometimes ᴛ̵ʜėr′fōr), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therfore, therfor, tharfore, thorfore, thorvore'' (= OFries. ''therfore'' (= D. ''daarvoor'' = MLG. ''darvore'' = G. ''dafür'' = Sw. ''derför'' = Dan. ''derfor''); < ''there'' + ''fore''. Cf. ''therefor''.] 1†. For that; for this; for it; therefor.

{{smaller block|Also, that alle the costages that be mad aboute hym be mad good of the box, ȝif he were nat of power to paie ''therfore'' hymself. {{right|''English Gilds'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|We fetched her round at last. Thank the Lord ''there-''}}

{{smaller block|Thus may ''ther-gatis'' be mente.
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 95.}}}}

'''therehence†''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-hens′), ''adv.'' [< ''there'' + ''hence''.] From that place, or from that circumstance; thence; also, on that account.

{{smaller block|Hauing gone through France, hee went ''therehence'' into Egypt. {{right|''Hakluyt's Voyages'', II. 4.}}
''Therehence'', they say, he was named the son of Amittai.
{{right|''Bp. John King'', On Jonah, p. 9.}}}}

'''therein''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-in′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therinne, ther-ynne, thærinne, thrinne, thrin'', < AS. ''thǣrinne'' (= OS. ''tharinna'' = OFries. ''therin'' = D. ''daarin'' = MLG. ''darinne'' = MHG. ''darīn, drīn'', G. ''darin'' = Sw. ''derinne'' = Dan. ''derinde''), < ''thǣr'', there, + ''inne'', in: see ''there''<sup>1</sup> and ''in''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. In that place, time, or thing.

{{smaller block|And [I] sawe a toure, as ich trowede, truth was ''ther-ynne.''
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), i. 15.}}
To thee all Angels cry aloud; the Heavens, and all the Powers ''therein.'' {{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Te Deum.}}}}

2. In that particular point or respect.

{{smaller block|''Therein'' thou wrong'st thy children mightily.
{{right|''Shak.'', 3 Hen. VI., iii. 2. 74.}}}}

'''thereinafter''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-in′af′tėr), ''adv.'' [< ''therein'' + ''after''.] Afterward in the same document; later on in the same instrument.

'''thereinbefore''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-in′bē-fōr′), ''adv.'' [< ''therein'' + ''before''.] Earlier in the same document; at a previous point in the same instrument.

'''thereinto''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-in′tö), ''adv.'' [< ''there'' + ''into''.] Into that, or into that place.

{{smaller block|Let them which are in Judæa flee to the mountains; . . . and let not them that are in the countries enter ''thereinto.'' {{right|Luke xxi. 21.}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|core.{{right|''Tennyson'', Queen Mary, iv. 3.}}}}

2†. In return or recompense for this or for that.

{{smaller block|We have forsaken all, and followed thee; what shall we have ''therefore?''{{right|Mat. xix. 27.}}}}

{{smaller block|An if I could [tell], what should I get ''therefore?''{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 2. 78.}}}}

3†. For that purpose or cause.

{{smaller block|Thei anoynten here Hondes and here Feet with a juyce made of Snayles and of othere thinges, made ''therfore.''{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 169.}}}}

{{smaller block|Thei wende verily that fendes were fallen a-mong the hoste. But thei were so bolde and so chiualrouse that ''ther-fore'' thei wolde not be discounfited.{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 625.}}}}

4. For this or for that reason; on that account: referring to something previously stated; consequently; by consequence.

{{smaller block|In Normandy there's little or no Wine at all grows, ''therefore'' the common Drink of that Country is Cyder.{{right|''Howell'', Letters, ii. 54.}}}}

{{smaller block|I have married a wife, and ''therefore'' I cannot come.{{right|Luke xiv. 20.}}}}

{{smaller block|The largeness of this short text [Render ''therefore'' to all men their dues] consists in that word ''therefore''; ''therefore'' because you have been so particularly taught your particular duties, ''therefore'' perform them, ''therefore'' practise them.{{right|''Donne'', Sermons, ix.}}}}

{{smaller block|He blushes; ''therefore'' he is guilty.{{right|''Spectator.''}}}}

{{smaller block|Line for line and point for point, your dominion is as great as theirs, though without fine names. Build, ''therefore'', your own world.{{right|''Emerson'', Nature, p. 92.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 4.''' ''Therefore, Wherefore, Accordingly, Consequently, Then, So.'' All these words draw a conclusion or infer}}

'''theremid†''', ''adv.'' [ME. ''thermid'', ''tharmid'', ''thormid''; < ''there'' + ''mid''<sup>2</sup>.] Therewith.

{{smaller block|He bad Bette go kutte a bowh other tweye,<br>And bete Beton ''ther-myd'' bote hue wolde worche.{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (C), vi. 136.}}}}

'''thereness''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr′nes), ''n.'' [< ''there'' + ''-ness.''] The quality of having location, situation, or existence with respect to some specified point or place.

{{smaller block|Could that possibly be the feeling of any special whereness or ''thereness?''{{right|''W. James'', Mind, XII. 18.}}}}

'''thereof''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-ov′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therof'', ''thereoffe'', ''tharof'' (= OFries. ''therof'' = Sw. Dan. ''deraf''); < ''there'' + ''of.''] 1. Of that; of it.

{{smaller block|In that partie is a Welle, that in the day it is so cold that no man may drynke ''there offe.''{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 156.}}}}

{{smaller block|In the day that thou eatest ''thereof'', thou shalt surely die.{{right|Gen. ii. 17.}}}}

2†. From that circumstance or cause.

{{smaller block|It seems his sleeps were hinder'd by thy railing,<br>And ''thereof'' comes it that his head is light.{{right|''Shak.'', C. of E., v. 1. 72.}}}}

'''thereologist''' (ther-ẹ̄-ol′ọ̄-jist), ''n.'' [< ''thereolog-y'' + ''-ist.''] One who is versed in thereology.

'''thereology''' (ther-ẹ̄-ol′ọ̄-ji), ''n.'' [Irreg. < Gr. θέρειν for θεραπεύειν, serve, attend (the sick), + -λογία, < λέγειν, speak: see ''-ology.''] The art of healing; therapeutics.

'''thereon''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-on′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''theron'', ''tharon'', ''therone'' (= OFries. ''theron'', ''deron'' = D. ''daaraan''

readings.c2s1:
= MLG. ''daran'' = OHG. ''dārana'', MHG. ''dār ane'', G. ''daran''); < ''there'' + ''on''<sup>1</sup>.] On that.

{{smaller block|Lyme and gravel comyxt ''thereon'' thou glide.
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|These arm'd him in blue arms, and gave a shield
Blue also, and ''thereon'' the morning star.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

'''thereout''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-out′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''thereoute'', ''theroute'', ''therute''; < ''there'' + ''out''.] 1. Out of that.

{{smaller block|Therefore fall the people unto them, and ''thereout'' suck they no small advantage.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Psalter, Ps. lxxiii. 10.}}}}

2. On the outside; out of doors; without. [Obsolete or Scotch.]

{{smaller block|And alle the walles beth of Wit to hold Wil ''thereoute''.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), vi. 77.}}}}

{{smaller block|Voydeth your man, and let him be ''theroute''.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Canon's Yeoman's Tale, l. 125.}}}}

3†. In consequence of that; as an outcome of that; therefore.

{{smaller block|And ''thereout'' have condemned them to lose their lives.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, iii.}}}}

'''thereover†''', ''adv.'' [< ME. ''therover'', ''tharover'' (= D. ''daarover'' = MLG. ''darover'' = G. ''darüber'' = Sw. ''deröfver'' = Dan. ''derover''); < ''there'' + ''over''.] Over that.

{{smaller block|And over the same watir seynt Eline made a brygge of stone whiche ys yett ''ther over''.
{{right|''Torkington'', Diarie of Eng. Travell, p. 27.}}}}

'''there-right''' (ᴛ̵ʜãr-rīt′), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''there'' + ''right'', ''adv''.] 1. Straight forward. ''Halliwell''. [Prov. Eng.]—2. On the very spot; right there. ''Halliwell''. [Prov. Eng.]
